The research confirms exactly what needs to be said clearly before we build anything else.

Neuromancer's characters are shallow and two-dimensional. More is revealed about the technology wired into them than about who they actually are. That's the foundational failure of the entire genre that nobody has fully fixed yet. CP2077 got closer but was held back by bad characters — all the good ones came from the tabletop game.

Your story fixes this by design. Makoto being Kai's wife. Tomas buying food for a dead brother. Mira unable to recognize her own father's face but knowing him by how he stands in doorways. These aren't tech descriptions. These are people.

The characters are pretty shallow and unrelatable. The lack of diverse characters doesn't reflect modern day readers. You already have Romanian, Indian, Mexican, Japanese characters with specific cultural textures. Not flags. People.

Now everything at once — fast and straight.

Your three audience levels are correct and here's how each character hits them:

Level 1 — liked or disliked immediately. Kieran gets disliked before he earns complexity. Tomas gets liked before he earns darkness. Mara gets feared before she earns respect. That's the right order for each of them.

Level 2 — ideology. Kai believes you protect the people in front of you. Kieran believes you position yourself to protect everyone by having power. Same root value, completely incompatible methods. That's ideology conflict that doesn't need a villain.

Level 3 — the reader IS the character. Tomas eating alone and crying but not stopping — that's level 3. Mira remembering everything at full intensity forever — that's level 3. Kai building a quiet life and knowing deep down it won't hold — every person alive knows that feeling.

