The Expanse — what and where:

Not a named district. Not a city. The territory between and beyond both cities that belongs to no government, no corpo, no gang officially. Think of it as the negative space of the twin cities — everything the cities pushed out eventually ends up there. Nomad camps, illegal manufacturing, buried corpo dumping grounds, communities that couldn't afford to exist inside city limits. It surrounds both cities like a ring and connects to the bay on one side.

Within the Expanse there are specific zones people have informal names for. The Shelf — elevated ground overlooking Crale where nomads park semi-permanently. The Drain — low industrial land between both cities where the Harvest operates freely. The Salt — dried bay section that floods seasonally, only accessible certain months, where the most hidden things happen.

Districts of Veyline City — built around your model of backed by corpos, gangs, mercs, or hunters:

Altagracia — the Latino district. Named after a Dominican saint, repurposed by Mexican and Central American communities over generations. It's where Kai and Kieran's cultural roots live in the city. The Valentinos equivalent isn't here — this district doesn't have one dominant gang. It has families. Extended networks of people who came from the same places and kept their traditions intact. The food is real. The music bleeds out of windows at every hour. This is Heywood done better — not a gang district wearing cultural clothes but an actual community that gangs have to negotiate with.

Lungshan — the Long River Collective's territory. Named after a famous Taiwanese temple, repurposed by the Chinese community over decades. The architecture is layered — old buildings wrapped in new infrastructure, traditional storefronts underneath corpo advertising. The Collective runs the community services here. You want a doctor who doesn't ask questions, a school that still teaches something true, a meal that tastes like somewhere else — Lungshan. The danger is invisible because the order is real.

Ironside — industrial district, Ironclad veterans territory. Former shipbuilding infrastructure converted into everything else. The buildings are massive and permanent — these aren't temporary structures, they were built to last centuries and now they house a thousand informal economies. Atlas had contracts here before the war ended. Some of that infrastructure still runs on Atlas systems the company forgot to reclaim.

The Nave — the corpo district. Where Atlas Dynamics keeps its Veyline face. Glass and altitude. The streets are clean because cleaning crews work around the clock. The people here don't make eye contact with anyone who doesn't look like they belong. There's a church in the center of it — old, pre-corpo, still operating — that nobody has been able to legally demolish because of heritage protections. The Congregation has quietly claimed it as sacred territory. Corpos ignore this because fighting the Congregation is expensive and the church is not worth the trouble.

Saltmarsh — the forgotten district. Where funding was supposed to arrive and didn't. Half-built towers, construction equipment rusted into permanence, populations that moved in because nobody else wanted it. The Null Collective operates here — they chose it deliberately because the incomplete infrastructure means the city's surveillance grid has enormous blind spots. You can move through Saltmarsh without being seen. That's worth more than real estate.

Districts of Crale:

Fundão — Portuguese-influenced district, working class, Sung-Jin Biocorp's shadow is everywhere without a single logo visible. The food distribution centers here. The community clinics that are technically independent but run on Sung-Jin supplies. Nobody talks about it. Everyone depends on it.

Redline — the actual boundary district, split down the middle by the old territorial line from the corporate war that separated the twin cities. One side slightly more Veyline-adjacent, one side more Crale. People who live here have learned to exist in the gap. Best black market in both cities because both cities' enforcement agencies argue over jurisdiction and nothing gets done.

Ashgate — the Indian community district, where the character you're building lives in his history before the council. Not wealthy anymore — the wealth that came over got redistributed by circumstance, by corpo pressure, by the simple cost of surviving. The culture stayed. The food stayed. The music stayed. It's the most quietly beautiful district in Crale because beauty is what people hold onto when everything else gets taken.

The Shelf — not technically a district, officially unincorporated, but everyone calls it that. Elevated ground above Crale proper where nomads have parked semi-permanently for forty years. The Drift gang calls this home. The view of both cities from here is the best in the region. Tourists don't know about it. Everyone who matters does.

